      "content": "Critics often argue that strong unions led to economic inefficiency by causing wage and price rigidities, which they claim stifled business growth and innovation. Another common critique is that the power of unions created an entrenched, powerful elite within the labor movement itself, potentially contradicting democratic ideals. Some economists suggest that the demands and compromises made by unions could sometimes slow down productivity gains necessary for overall economic dynamism. These arguments generally focus on market distortions and centralized power dynamics.",
      "source": "target",
