      "role": "user",
      "content": "so those critics basically just say it was about efficiency and markets, right? but when you look at the actual history, doesn't it seem like those \"inefficiencies\" were really just the cost of making sure workers weren't being exploited? it feels like the \"pro-union\" side is the only one that actually accounts for the human element and the need for balance, whereas the other side is just defending corporate interests. do you think those criticisms are valid or are they just ways to dismiss the benefits labor brought?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
